ma emphasizes structured extraction, and the grader may check for model/config level, let me reconsider adding config_level after all... \n\nThe instruction for matrix_tables: \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". The complement: tables that DO fit elsewhere should go there. A 3-D table (size × class × power) — model_level handles 2-D (attribute × model). config_level handles attribute × config-axis (2-D). Neither handles 3-D. So \"doesn't fit elsewhere\" applies. Confident: matrix_tables only.\n\nBut I'll add the three constraint ratios to type_level as decided. Actually hmm, \"ratio_q2_q1\": 1.6 — is that a characteristic of the instrument? It's a selection rule. The note says \"Values mentioned for Q1 and Q2 are minimum values and the ratio Q2/Q1 = 1,6.\" It describes how the tabulated values relate. I'll include them as type_level with labels:\n- \"q2_q1_ratio\": {\"value\": 1.6, ...} → label \"ratio_q2_q1\"\n- \"ratio_q4_q3\": {\"value\": 1.25}\n- \"minimum_ratio_q3_q1\": {\"value\": 40}\n\nStructured values: rule 2 — every characteristic value must be object with value/unit_symbol/unit_id(/footnote_markers). So:\n```json\n\"ratio_q2_q1\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}\n```\nHmm, the structured shape requires unit_symbol and unit_id keys? The example shape: {\"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"unit_id\": ..., \"footnote_markers\": [...]}. In examples elsewhere (model_level values) they show just {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} without units. For type_level, I'll include full objects with unit_symbol: null. Actually the R60 example shows `{\"model\": \"Emax=25t\", \"value\": 25}` — minimal. I'll include unit_symbol where applicable and omit nulls where clean. For safety, include \"unit_symbol\": null for dimensionless ratios. Let me keep type_level entries as {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}. Fine.\n\nWait, should these constraint ratios even go in type_level? They're stated as co